WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.480
So I thought the film was really entertaining,
there were a lot of big, action-packed moments,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.970
but some really nice tender moments as well.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.959
I have to ask - what was it like working with
not one, but THREE giant CGI animals?

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:16.520
Erm well one of them was really fun, because
George was played by an actor called Jason

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:22.350
Liles, so we had a lot to kind of react to
and he gave us a lot of emotion and so on...

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.680
And a lot of humour as well, so that was great.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.120
But the other two were just tennis balls.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.120
Wow.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.120
That was it.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.120
Tennis balls on sticks.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.090
That's all we had to go off.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:36.510
And that was tough because you have to just
use your imagination, thankfully our director,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:41.690
Brad Payton, he's an amazing painter, so he
did these sketches of what the creatures were

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:46.399
going to look like but having them on a piece
of paper this size is not the same as seeing

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:49.010
them, like, ginormous.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.820
And they're amazing on screen as well, seriously
impressive, especially when the crocodile

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.609
comes out of the water - it's a big moment.

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:57.399
I like the flying wolf.

00:00:57.399 --> 00:01:00.309
I didn't think he got as much screen time
as he deserved - I'm a dog person.

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:01.909
Yeah that's true actually, oh yeah.

00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:03.820
I was rooting for the wolf.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.280
So your character is a little bit scatty,
she's really witty, and she's got a really

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.580
nice heart.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.750
Was she quite an enjoyable character to play?

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.970
She really was, I love the fact that she's
badass as well, you know, I love the fact

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:21.500
that she's also really brave, she's kind of
like, okay I feel responsible for this virus

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.460
situation so I'm going to dive right in there
and put my life at risk and go and try and

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.460
save the world.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.520
Because you know it's totally different to
me.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.860
I'd be staying at home, hiding...

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:32.860
Watching it on the news?

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.510
Yeah watching it on the news, exactly.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:39.900
The film is obviously a really big production,
there's a lot of stunts, did you have to undergo

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.200
any special training, or up your workouts
or switch stuff up?

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.620
So I did up my workouts, and I did sort of
work with the lady who actually prepared me

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.103
for the Bond franchise, so I worked with her
for a month before I went, but I might as

00:01:53.103 --> 00:01:57.380
well have not bothered, to be honest because
it was not anything to do with stunts.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.520
Oh really?

00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.520
No!

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.820
It wasn't stuff that I could train for, it
was things like being strung up to the ceiling

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:10.060
on wires and being on a replica plane, tilted
at 90 degrees and having to swing from one

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.810
side of it to the other.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.930
And crawl along with a wind machine blowing
in your face and, things like that really,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:20.590
so I guess my workout really helped, but it
wasn't those kind of stunts like, in Bond

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.060
or what have you, it wasn't like that.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:29.010
And then it was a lot of green screen stunts,
like in the helicopter and so on.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.330
I think RAMPAGE is actually quite an inspiring
film - it kind of makes you want to get out

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.870
of bed and go and save the world...

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:35.870
Really?

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:36.870
I love that!

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:37.870
I haven't heard that before, I love it...

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.590
Who in your life inspires you the most?

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.942
Erm, I would say my mum inspires me the most.

00:02:42.942 --> 00:02:49.420
I think, you know, she had me when she was
18 years old so she was very young and she

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:55.561
raised me on her own and erm despite, you
know, we didn't come from money at all, but

00:02:55.561 --> 00:02:59.020
despite that my mum always taught me that
anything is possible and I could achieve anything

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.020
that I put my mind to.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.020
That's so sweet.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.800
And I really am just so grateful to her for
that.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.459
As an actress you obviously do a lot of travelling,
there's a lot of long days, do you have any

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:10.920
wellness hacks that you absolutely swear by?

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.940
Oh my God, so many.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:14.940
SO many.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:20.520
I feel like waking up before 6am is really
important, that's really important because

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:25.319
it's good to wake up with the sun and I find
that the longer I stay in bed, the more kind

00:03:25.319 --> 00:03:28.980
of, sleepy I am throughout the day I mean.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:33.750
And then also, it means that I have, I carve
out time for myself to kind of do my routines,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.980
which are: I like to do a bit of exercise
to get my heart rate pumping, nothing like

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.760
Dwayne's workout, not his two hours in the
gym.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.300
And then I also meditate and then I do my
morning pages as well.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.240
So I just kind of write whatever comes into
my head, kind of my mini diary and that's

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:56.690
a real great way of, things that I'm uncomfortable
with or I need to sort of dissect in my mind

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:58.830
I get them on the page and it really helps
me.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.190
That's amazing, I might try that.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:04.739
And I'm sure our audience would love to know
which beauty products could you absolutely

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:05.739
not live without?

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:07.970
I think the biggest one for me is cleanser.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:11.130
So many of my friends go to sleep with their
makeup on.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.130
Oh my gosh.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:13.130
And I'm like, [gasp].

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:14.130
Cardinal sin!

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.130
Oh my gosh!

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.750
Like it's really bad.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.900
So I would, I have to have cleanser.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:25.199
I have to cleanse my face, especially after
a day like this with so much makeup on, in

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:27.560
front of the camera and lights all day, you
know.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:28.560
Is there a specific brand that you love?

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.380
I love The Organic Pharmacy, I love Environ,
as well, I'm a big fan of Environ.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:34.550
Those are my two favourites.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:35.550
Amazing.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.470
Well, thank you very much for your time.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:39.330
Thank you.

